#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76573843-ec78-585b-af82-bae778b83009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331055} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2811c8-62aa-5a93-8981-18721a20ad59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331056} {:answer/id #uuid "83eed442-106f-5182-8a04-9b0e401c10cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331057} {:answer/id #uuid "24db7740-142f-5ff4-ad09-89cffbccbd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331058} {:answer/id #uuid "08936e67-0d0d-5760-a873-7da74707acef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83eed442-106f-5182-8a04-9b0e401c10cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331057} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f798868-eff2-5e73-9471-09264f27a8c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "185f66f3-9fba-5841-9ae6-a31944a1af0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b295344e-e699-5f9b-973c-133bb94797ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331062} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbaef38-07e5-5120-922c-f92b0bf12ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331063} {:answer/id #uuid "8184a3fd-fd88-58f2-baeb-76fb0f7d30ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331064} {:answer/id #uuid "688b0570-33d1-547f-88ac-8355397694b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331065} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0a8753-ae01-5ea7-a98c-333a2b9c7968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8184a3fd-fd88-58f2-baeb-76fb0f7d30ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331064} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f8131f-da98-5ca7-8fec-b89d5f9e624f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c83000c4-b29e-5b4d-9073-cd5d94e04a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-131115" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cd2d07-59f1-5c9d-909e-2170951b37aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331069} {:answer/id #uuid "d78e9b94-7053-513c-889e-9ac4ee996a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331070} {:answer/id #uuid "16b5ac7f-7e44-532e-9c98-2ab0811a023b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331071} {:answer/id #uuid "b1be908a-3ae9-5e12-9823-793330a24909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331072} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c38b2c-71a1-501f-83d1-68f8363a1b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d78e9b94-7053-513c-889e-9ac4ee996a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331070} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f3b0ae0-9f9e-5e3a-9686-db4660f93548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07199144-954c-5fb9-9281-fbd6b578758f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331076} {:answer/id #uuid "80488e4e-dae7-543e-92a0-ba47ca6c9b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331077} {:answer/id #uuid "d383307d-39b2-5b44-a0bb-c6ed56a25ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331078} {:answer/id #uuid "327e391f-c2db-5954-82f9-0f504597cf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331079} {:answer/id #uuid "24d75cf5-e417-5687-a614-6b11aab26878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80488e4e-dae7-543e-92a0-ba47ca6c9b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331077} :answer-field/id #uuid "52507be3-308b-5474-b419-8aabeb3a8d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e82fe7da-02e3-534e-99f6-4b0ee233facf" :question/math-academy-id "q-71388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b31a0be-80ae-57dc-9940-d27931890588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331083} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f837b1-7d33-57e9-a9cb-31287c104b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331084} {:answer/id #uuid "6a987c18-a4a8-520c-b8f6-674b3f53509a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331085} {:answer/id #uuid "12ea20d2-3502-57b7-9b33-7479f31d787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331086} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6490d1-5aa9-5055-a31d-9d46b58528f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c6490d1-5aa9-5055-a31d-9d46b58528f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331087} :answer-field/id #uuid "447c3cd3-d9c5-5114-8434-345243d995fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71407" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}]}
